The coalesced sum of two approximable maps relates a coalesced neighbourhood W to a coalesced neighbourhood W' when W' is the separated-sum master, or both are proper left copies related by the first map, or both are proper right copies related by the second map. Intersecting on the right, a master absorbs the other neighbourhood, two left copies intersect by intersecting the related left neighbourhoods, and two right copies do likewise, while one neighbourhood cannot be both a left copy and a right copy. For monotonicity, a coalesced neighbourhood contained in a left injection is a proper left copy and one contained in a right injection is a proper right copy; a larger codomain that is the master remains the master, a same-side enlargement follows by monotonicity of the component map, and a cross from left to right is impossible.
